<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hello, Tony, old mate. I\'92m sorry I can\'92t be with you right now, and, look, now that I\'92ve pressed the record button, I\'92m fighting every reactive impulse to tell you all this in person, but I know it wouldn\'92t serve you, it wouldn\'92t serve you well to carry this until it\'92s time. The more you know, the more in danger you\'92ll be. And the sooner you know it, the sooner and deeper the danger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So, here I am, recording a vid, hoping for the best, planning for the worst. I know this all seems very tropey, my ol\'92 mate, but if you\'92re watching this now, it probably means I\'92ve gone to the meet the great Serial Killer in the Sky.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>I suppose I should start with a confession. You may have suspected this all the long, I dunno, but I do get a sense you\'92re smarter than you make out. Certainly your lineage would lend credence to that, but I digress. First, the confession \'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Right, here we go. I know we\'92ve grown to be best friends, but it didn\'92t start that way I\'92m sorry to say. In the beginning, I was assigned to protect you, to stop \'85 well, yeah \'85 to keep you alive, brother. My true name is not Haloren Smythe, and it benefits no one to speak my real name \'85 to be honest I\'92m not even sure what it is any more \'85 so many lies, Tony, so many lies. The things is, I\'92ve lied to you, and that\'92s about the worst thing a man could do to another man \'85 I mean, women! Women lie all the time, they have to, my old mate, to compensate for all their other weaknesses. Not like us men. We have strength of character and should always tell the truth, at least among men we trust.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So, I\'92m sorry I haven\'92t been truthful with you, but I\'92ll hope you\'92ll come to understand it was my job to keep you in the dark about so many things while I kept those other dumb PAPER agents from wiping you from the face of the earth. And in case you\'92re wondering, they were the taxing carpet lickers who blew up the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 , just to kill you! That\'92s how demented this has all become. There were other times too when they tried to kill you, like that time at Floormart when they almost tipped a load of gym equipment straight down on you. Sure, it looked like an accident\'97Floormart customers are always dying during deliveries; Lawn Mower week is the worst. If I hadn\'92t got to the truck driver at the last second, kicked him out of the cabin and yanked the steering wheel, you would\'92ve died under an avalanche of Kleenanjerk home weightlifting sets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Like any calcified old intelligence agency, PAPER\'92s left hand never knows what testicle the right hand is scratching, or even if one department is gonna rim another department, or fist it till it bleeds. We\'92re supposed to be on the same side, Tony, but there are so many sides and so many angles to play and an agent who doesn\'92t at least consider all the angles is a dead agent. Like that bitch Avi Grubler \'85 she nearly got you, brother, with that bomb of hers, and I dunno how you escaped because I know I took my eye off you for just a minute \'85 I was so hungry, brother. That bitch Sarah Jacobson made me so damn hungry, brother; she played on my appetites and she called me out, and she made me so hungry, I just \'85 I\'92m sorry I almost got you killed. I dunno who got you out of there in time, but please thank him for me, will you?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><<Tony wonders why Halo wouldn\'92t recall it was Sarah who saved him, with the ruse of going out to dinner>>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yeah so that\'92s the confession: I\'92m deep cover PAPER agent, assigned to keep you alive. And I nearly failed you, brother, so please forgive me. Please forgive me, as a man among men, as you most certainly are.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While we\'92re on the subject of Sarah Jacobson, lemme just tell you, because I love you, brother, you know I do. I never bought that she was your girlfriend. Stacies like that never go out with men like you; they have no appreciation for an honest stand-up guy like you or me, the kind of men with traditional family values. Women like her don\'92t accept  the natural order of things. They never accept responsibility for all the social upheaval and dislocation they cause, just be thinking they\'92re equal to us. Mate, if they\'92re our equal, just let them go fifteen rounds with us, that\'92s what I say. Women like that never want a normal, stable relationship. They\'92re always trying to gain a footing they don\'92t deserve. They\'92re underhanded and manipulative, they try to control but in dishonourable ways, ways you never see coming. They want to own us and try to bring us down to their level. Who do they think they are, our mothers? Well, tax that. We already got one mother each and that\'92s already one mother too many. We don\'92t need any of them undermining their sons to get revenge on their husbands. The sooner we can breed men in test-tubes, the sooner we can put women in their place. Farm them, I say, and when we need a new one, we just go to market and get a new one. Or two, or as many as you like. And when we\'92re finished with them, we just sell them for pig feed. Circle of life, brother.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Anyway, I digress, but I just needed to get that off my chest, because I love you, and I\'92ll always be on your side. So I just need to warn you: stay away from that Sarah Jacobson. She too perfect for anyone\'92s good, and\'97don\'92t take this the wrong way, brother, I\'92m just calling out the way the natural order of things is\'97she\'92s too good for you. She knows it, I know it, and I hope you know. If she wants to be in a relationship with you, she\'92s up to something. I\'92m sorry I have to say it, but it\'92s only because I care about you.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Anyway, look, I\'92m rambling and I wanted to keep this simple. I\'92ve put a lot of information on this drive, but you won\'92t be able to access until \'85 well, you\'92ll know when.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Right now, I will give you two things. The rest will fall into place later. Appearing at the bottom of the screen now is an address. I need you to go there right away and follow the instructions that the thumb drive is now loading onto your tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s all I got for now. I\'92ll see you when you get there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now that time has stopped for me, I will always be your friend.\
